{
  "dom_id": "step-q367472",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x)^{′}=5{x}^{4}+6x-1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}+6x-1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}+6)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1)}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.5)^{4}+1}{20(0.5)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.227\\,941….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.227\\,941…)^{4}+1}{20(0.227\\,941…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,829…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{2}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.166\\,829…)^{4}+1}{20(0.166\\,829…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,033….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.17$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.17$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.17,$ rounded to two decimal places.",
